            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-41992",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GNU gzip contains a global buffer overflow vulnerability in the LZH de ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GNU gzip contains a global buffer overflow vulnerability in the LZH decompression logic caused by improper reuse of shared global state between different decompression formats within a single execution. GNU gzip maintains a global array that is shared across the LZ77, LZW, and LZH decompression routines and is not reinitialized between files processed in the same invocation.\nBy decompressing a specially crafted LZW file followed by a specially crafted LZH file in a single gzip -d command, an attacker can poison the shared global state and subsequently trigger an out‑of‑bounds read in the LZH decoder. The LZH decompression logic follows stale values left in the shared array, causing reads past the end of the allocated global buffer.\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit 63dbf6b3b9e6e781df1a6a64e609b10e23969681"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-41992\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: gzip\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-41992](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-41992)\nGNU gzip contains a global buffer overflow vulnerability in the LZH decompression logic caused by improper reuse of shared global state between different decompression formats within a single execution. GNU gzip maintains a global array that is shared across the LZ77, LZW, and LZH decompression routines and is not reinitialized between files processed in the same invocation.\nBy decompressing a specially crafted LZW file followed by a specially crafted LZH file in a single gzip -d command, an attacker can poison the shared global state and subsequently trigger an out‑of‑bounds read in the LZH decoder. The LZH decompression logic follows stale values left in the shared array, causing reads past the end of the allocated global buffer.\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit 63dbf6b3b9e6e781df1a6a64e609b10e23969681"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-41992",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-41991",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "gzip: gzip: Arbitrary file overwrite via insecure temporary file handling in gzexe utility"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GNU gzip contains a vulnerability in the gzexe utility related to insecure temporary file handling. When the mktemp utility is not available in the user’s PATH, gzexe falls back to constructing a temporary file path based solely on the process ID (PID). This predictable filename is created without exclusive access or existence checks.\nA local attacker can pre‑create the predicted temporary file path as a symbolic link pointing to an arbitrary file writable by the victim. When gzexe runs, it follows the symlink and overwrites the target file, resulting in a time‑of‑check to time‑of‑use (TOCTOU) condition that allows arbitrary file overwrite.\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit 4e6f8b24ab823146ab8776f0b7fe486ab34d4269"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-41991\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: gzip\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-41991](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-41991)\nGNU gzip contains a vulnerability in the gzexe utility related to insecure temporary file handling. When the mktemp utility is not available in the user’s PATH, gzexe falls back to constructing a temporary file path based solely on the process ID (PID). This predictable filename is created without exclusive access or existence checks.\nA local attacker can pre‑create the predicted temporary file path as a symbolic link pointing to an arbitrary file writable by the victim. When gzexe runs, it follows the symlink and overwrites the target file, resulting in a time‑of‑check to time‑of‑use (TOCTOU) condition that allows arbitrary file overwrite.\n\nThis issue has been fixed in the commit 4e6f8b24ab823146ab8776f0b7fe486ab34d4269"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-41991",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-54369",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "acl: Symlink traversal privilege escalation via libacl functions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "acl before version 2.4.0 contains a symlink traversal vulnerability in the libacl pathname-based functions acl_get_file(), acl_set_file(), acl_extended_file(), and acl_delete_def_file() that allows local attackers to escalate privileges by replacing any pathname component with a symbolic link. Attackers who control any component of a pathname processed by a privileged caller can redirect ACL read or write operations to arbitrary files or directories, enabling unauthorized manipulation of access control lists and local privilege escalation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-54369\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: libacl1\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-54369](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-54369)\nacl before version 2.4.0 contains a symlink traversal vulnerability in the libacl pathname-based functions acl_get_file(), acl_set_file(), acl_extended_file(), and acl_delete_def_file() that allows local attackers to escalate privileges by replacing any pathname component with a symbolic link. Attackers who control any component of a pathname processed by a privileged caller can redirect ACL read or write operations to arbitrary files or directories, enabling unauthorized manipulation of access control lists and local privilege escalation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-54369",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-54370",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "acl: TOCTOU Symlink Traversal via getfacl/setfacl"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "acl before version 2.4.0 contains a time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) race condition vulnerability that allows local attackers to escalate privileges by replacing a pathname component with a symbolic link between an lstat() check and subsequent symlink-following operations such as stat(), chown(), chmod(), acl_get_file(), and acl_set_file(). Attackers who control a pathname component can redirect file access control list operations to arbitrary files when getfacl, setfacl, or chacl is invoked by a privileged process over an attacker-controlled path, resulting in local privilege escalation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-54370\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: libacl1\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-54370](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-54370)\nacl before version 2.4.0 contains a time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) race condition vulnerability that allows local attackers to escalate privileges by replacing a pathname component with a symbolic link between an lstat() check and subsequent symlink-following operations such as stat(), chown(), chmod(), acl_get_file(), and acl_set_file(). Attackers who control a pathname component can redirect file access control list operations to arbitrary files when getfacl, setfacl, or chacl is invoked by a privileged process over an attacker-controlled path, resulting in local privilege escalation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-54370",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-54371",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "attr: attr: Symlink Traversal Privilege Escalation via getfattr and setfattr"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "attr before version 2.6.0 contains a symlink traversal vulnerability in the getfattr and setfattr utilities that allows local attackers to escalate privileges by replacing a pathname component with a symbolic link during directory hierarchy traversal. Attackers who control a pathname component can redirect getfattr and setfattr operations to arbitrary files by substituting a symlink, leading to local privilege escalation when getfattr or setfattr is invoked by a privileged process over an attacker-controlled path."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-54371\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: libattr1\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-54371](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-54371)\nattr before version 2.6.0 contains a symlink traversal vulnerability in the getfattr and setfattr utilities that allows local attackers to escalate privileges by replacing a pathname component with a symbolic link during directory hierarchy traversal. Attackers who control a pathname component can redirect getfattr and setfattr operations to arbitrary files by substituting a symlink, leading to local privilege escalation when getfattr or setfattr is invoked by a privileged process over an attacker-controlled path."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-54371",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-42250",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "bzip2: bzip2: Denial of Service in bzip2recover via a specially crafted file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "bzip2 contains an off‑by‑one error in the bzip2recover utility. When processing a specially crafted file, the application performs an out‑of‑bounds write to a global buffer, resulting in memory corruption and a crash (denial of service).\n\nThis issue was fixed in bzip2 patch 35d122a3df8b0cc4082a4d89fdc6ee99f375fe67"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-42250\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: libbz2-1.0\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-42250](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-42250)\nbzip2 contains an off‑by‑one error in the bzip2recover utility. When processing a specially crafted file, the application performs an out‑of‑bounds write to a global buffer, resulting in memory corruption and a crash (denial of service).\n\nThis issue was fixed in bzip2 patch 35d122a3df8b0cc4082a4d89fdc6ee99f375fe67"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-42250",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-0395",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "glibc: buffer overflow in the GNU C Library's assert()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When the assert() function in the GNU C Library versions 2.13 to 2.40 fails, it does not allocate enough space for the assertion failure message string and size information, which may lead to a buffer overflow if the message string size aligns to page size."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-0395\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: libc6\nFixed Version: 2.36-9+deb12u10\nLink: [CVE-2025-0395](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-0395)\nWhen the assert() function in the GNU C Library versions 2.13 to 2.40 fails, it does not allocate enough space for the assertion failure message string and size information, which may lead to a buffer overflow if the message string size aligns to page size."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-0395",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
