            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-7017",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP::Tiny versions before 0.095 for Perl forward credential headers t ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP::Tiny versions before 0.095 for Perl forward credential headers to cross-origin redirect targets.\n\nWhen the server returns a 3xx redirect, `_maybe_redirect` follows the `Location:` header and `_prepare_headers_and_cb` re-merges the caller's `headers` argument into the new request, without checking whether the redirect target shares an origin with the original URL. Caller-supplied `Authorization`, `Cookie` and `Proxy-Authorization` headers are therefore re-sent to whatever host the redirect names, across scheme, host or port boundaries, and including `https` to `http` downgrades that expose them in plaintext on the wire.\n\nThe HTTP::Tiny POD note that \"Authorization headers will not be included in a redirected request\" applied only to the URL-userinfo Basic-auth path, not to headers passed explicitly by the caller."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-7017\nSeverity: UNKNOWN\nPackage: perl-base\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-7017](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-7017)\nHTTP::Tiny versions before 0.095 for Perl forward credential headers to cross-origin redirect targets.\n\nWhen the server returns a 3xx redirect, `_maybe_redirect` follows the `Location:` header and `_prepare_headers_and_cb` re-merges the caller's `headers` argument into the new request, without checking whether the redirect target shares an origin with the original URL. Caller-supplied `Authorization`, `Cookie` and `Proxy-Authorization` headers are therefore re-sent to whatever host the redirect names, across scheme, host or port boundaries, and including `https` to `http` downgrades that expose them in plaintext on the wire.\n\nThe HTTP::Tiny POD note that \"Authorization headers will not be included in a redirected request\" applied only to the URL-userinfo Basic-auth path, not to headers passed explicitly by the caller."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-7017",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-5958",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "sed: GNU sed TOCTOU race condition"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When sed is invoked with both -i (in-place edit) and --follow-symlinks, the function open_next_file() performs two separate, non-atomic filesystem operations on the same path: \n1. resolves symlink to its target and stores the resolved path for determining when output is written,\n2. opens the original symlink path (not the resolved one) to read the file. \nBetween these two calls there is a race window. If an attacker atomically replaces the symlink with a different target during that window, sed will: read content from the new (attacker-chosen) symlink target and write the processed result to the path recorded in step 1. This can lead to arbitrary file overwrite with attacker-controlled content in the context of the sed process.\n\n\nThis issue was fixed in version 4.10."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-5958\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: sed\nFixed Version: 4.9-1+deb12u1\nLink: [CVE-2026-5958](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-5958)\nWhen sed is invoked with both -i (in-place edit) and --follow-symlinks, the function open_next_file() performs two separate, non-atomic filesystem operations on the same path: \n1. resolves symlink to its target and stores the resolved path for determining when output is written,\n2. opens the original symlink path (not the resolved one) to read the file. \nBetween these two calls there is a race window. If an attacker atomically replaces the symlink with a different target during that window, sed will: read content from the new (attacker-chosen) symlink target and write the processed result to the path recorded in step 1. This can lead to arbitrary file overwrite with attacker-controlled content in the context of the sed process.\n\n\nThis issue was fixed in version 4.10."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-5958",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "scanner:Trivy"
                ]
              }
            },
